   "claims": [
    "Within nuclear physics, superscripts are placed before symbols to denote the atomic mass of isotopes, such as 14 C 14C for Carbon-14.",
    "Math is the easiest one: superscripts are most commonly employed to write exponents, like in the Pythagorean theorem, which we’ve already seen, or in a quadratic equation: 𝑎 𝑥 2 + 𝑏 𝑥 + 𝑐 = 0 ax2+bx+c=0.",
    "The unique character for a 2 in superscript is “²”.",
    "All 10 digits are available as superscript characters, as well as some symbols and most lowercase and uppercase letters, aside from q and Q."
   ],
